ilver medal went to German " [:a {:href "/athletes/920587"} "Rudolf Binding"] " for his " [:em {} "Reitvorschrift für eine Geliebte"] " (A Rider’s Instructions to His Lover). The script consisted of “a series of 45 designated short, highly stylized prose lyricism”, as the author stated. The sections were called, for example, ‘horse chit-chat’, ‘Riding is saying yes’, ‘Choice of the horse’, ‘Ride from the farm’, ‘Action of the horse’ or ‘The horse of Mohammed’. The script was reprinted several times after his death. The cover was designed by " [:a {:href "/athletes/920722"} "Renée Sintenis"] ", who later also designed the Berlin Bear, nowadays the symbol of the German capital Berlin."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Danish poet " [:a {:href "/athletes/920249"} "Johannes Weltzer"] " wrote a poem about " [:a {:href "/athletes/1200010"} "Charles Lindbergh"] " and received the bronze medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Catalogue Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Translated Title"] [:th {} "Original Title"] [:th {} "Title in Olympic Report or Catalogue"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/921250"} "Kazimierz Wierzyński"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/POL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/POL.png"}] "POL"]] [:td {} "Olympic Laurel"] [:td {} "Laur Olimpijski"] [:td {} "Laur Olimpijski"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/920587"} "Rudolf Binding"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "A Rider's Instruction to His Lover"] [:td {} "Reitvorschrift für eine Gelie